+### @@PD-164@@ — the layering is a table of what each module may import
+**2026-10-18, cycle 0.0.1 step 1.**
+
+*What was read* — `BUILD.md` B-18 stated the layering as a drawing and the sentence "a module may not import a
+module to its left". The drawing gave `option` no arrow, though `OPTION_MODEL.md` P-1's generic form is the
+`setsockopt` call, whose number `src/sys/` holds (`SOCKET_MODEL.md` §1; `TESTING.md`'s `check_constants_named`), and
+it gave `socket` no arrow to `addr`, though `SOCKET_MODEL.md` K-13's `bind` takes the address encoder's bytes and
+K-14 records the bound address. The sentence read the drawing's columns, in which `addr` stands under `socket`, and
+`datagram` and `ancillary` stand left of `server`, so that read literally it let `datagram` import `server`.
+
+*So:* B-18 is a table, one row per module, naming every module it may import directly — what the drawing's arrows
+reach from it, with those two arrows added, since `use` is not transitive (B-17) and a module that names a `core`
+type imports `core` itself. `src/README.md` points at the table, each placeholder module states its row in its
+header (cycle 0.0.1's plan), and the harness's `check_layering` (cycle 0.0.3) reads it. The one place a
+specification already disagrees with it is held as `O-K4`, for cycle 0.2: `SOCKET_MODEL.md` K-12 has the builder,
+which is `src/socket/`'s, return `TcpListener` and `TcpStream`, which are `src/stream/`'s.
+
+*Alternatives declined:* **add the two arrows and keep the drawing** — the sentence would still read columns, which a
+check cannot read and a reader can misread; **let `socket` import `stream`** — a `use` cycle with `stream`'s import
+of `socket`, legal and a decomposition mistake (B-19).
+
+*Reversal:* none needed for the table, which changes no import any file makes — `src/` held none before this
+subcycle; a cycle that needs an import its module's row lacks amends the row by a decision.
+
+### @@PD-165@@ — the generic option form lives in `src/option/sockopt.npk`, because `raw` is a keyword
+**2026-10-18, cycle 0.0.1 step 1.**
+
+*What was measured* — a module named `raw` is `NITPICK-PARSE-001` at its `mod:` line at compiler `c9baa9e`: `raw` is
+a keyword, and from the compiler's landing 113 a keyword declared as a name of any kind is refused (cycle 0.0.0's
+block 0b; the early ecosystem audit's EC7). So `SK-026`'s `src/option/raw.npk` could never compile. A module named
+`sockopt` compiles there (cycle 0.0.1's block 0b).
+
+*So:* the generic form — the library's only `setsockopt` and `getsockopt` calls — is confined to
+`src/option/sockopt.npk`, named for the call it wraps. This supersedes in part `SK-026`, the file it names; its rule — typed options, one function
+each, the generic form confined and not re-exported — stands, and so does `check_no_raw_setsockopt`'s name, which
+names the call it bans. `OPTION_MODEL.md` P-1, `TESTING.md`'s check row, `ROADMAP.md`, the 0.7 and 1.0 roadmaps and
+`src/option/README.md` name the file.
+
+*Alternatives declined:* **`generic.npk`** — the word reads as the language's generics; **`untyped.npk`** — it names
+what the form lacks, not what it holds.
+
+*Reversal:* a rename, by a later decision; no rule moves.
